</table>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.SSx18.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SSx18.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Across the 32 Palantir donor/method jobs, 20 succeeded and 12 failed. Failures were dominated by “Palantir produced <math id="Sx1.SSx18.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math> terminal states” (10 cases), with one infinity-bearing run and one eigensolver non-convergence. Because no donor succeeded for all methods simultaneously, the strict all-method shared-donor intersection was empty. We therefore treat Palantir as a qualitative stress test rather than a decisive ranking benchmark.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SSx19" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">S19. Embedding ablation</h3>

<div id="Sx1.SSx19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SSx19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To determine whether the extracted head adds value beyond what is already present in scGPT’s native embeddings, we compared it against two frozen scGPT embedding readouts: the average-pooled contextual embedding (averaging over all token positions) and the <span id="Sx1.SSx19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;cls&gt;</span>-token embedding (a single summary vector from the model’s cell-processing checkpoint).</p>
</div>
